{ ... }:

# Settings for ONE machine.
#
# Copy to hosts/miner/local.nix on the rig that needs it. Nothing else imports
# it, and the other rigs simply do not have the file, so the generic image
# stays generic.
#
# Then `git add hosts/miner/local.nix`. Nix copies only *tracked* files out of
# a git working tree, so an untracked one is invisible: the import is skipped,
# the build succeeds, and the workaround silently does not happen. Check with
#
#   nix eval .#nixosConfigurations.miner.config.boot.kernelParams
#
# before you reboot a rig expecting the fix to be there.
#
# Only put things here that genuinely cannot be shared or detected at runtime.
# Firmware workarounds qualify: they have to be on the kernel command line
# before there is a kernel to detect anything with. A frequency ceiling does
# not -- that belongs in /etc/rig/max-freq-percent on the rig itself.
#
# Say which machine this is. In a fleet of identical configs, the comment is
# the only thing that will tell you a year from now.

{
  # Machine: ASUS Z170-DELUXE rev 1.xx, AMI BIOS 1302 (2015-11-09), i7-6700K.
  #
  # This board's DSDT declares \_GPE._L69 but never defines the \_GPE._L69.D1F0
  # that it calls. Left alone, GPE 0x69 re-arms in a loop on some cold boots and
  # the failing handler floods the console -- measured at ~100 lines in 76 ms,
  # which cost five aborted boots in 13 minutes, each dying 30-55 s in. Masking
  # it costs nothing: that handler was never able to run anyway.
  #
  # See modules/quirks.nix for how to tell whether your board has this.
  rig.quirks.maskGpes = [ "0x69" ];
}
